<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      The plate the system paints before this process runs, out of whatever night mode IT
      resolves for Flick — a pale window background would flash white before a near-black
      app. That resolution is Flick's own answer only while the appearance preference is
      Match system: from API 31 MainActivity hands the choice to UiModeManager, so the
      system reads this bucket off the app's own mode on every launch after the one the
      choice was made on, and a Dark user on a light phone lands here too. API 26-30 has
      no per-app night mode short of AppCompat, which this app does not use, so there this
      stays the phone's answer and MainActivity's repaint from the resolved palette is the
      first frame the preference owns. The status-bar icons are seeded from the same
      window; the navigation-bar ones are owned by FlickApp's insets controller instead of
      this theme, because they also have to invert per-route on the forced-cinematic
      screens.

      windowBackground is DarkFlickColors.canvas written out by hand — a resource the
      system reads before this process exists cannot be derived from a Kotlin value.
      ThemePlateTest holds the two together; retune the palette without this and the cold
      start opens on a band of the old colour.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.FlickSender" parent="@android:style/Theme.Material.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">#0C0F18</item>
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">false</item>
    </style>
</resources>
